# Beginner Sourdough Bread

Source: Grant Bakes, "How I'd Make Sourdough If I Could Start Over (For Beginners)". https://youtu.be/pQxuWfVnwyE

## Recipe Name: Beginner Sourdough Bread

## Servings: 1 loaf

## Prep Time: 45 mins active, plus overnight starter and 6 to 10 hrs rising

## Cook Time: 35 to 40 mins

## Ingredients:
### Starter Feed (the night before):
- 25 g unfed sourdough starter, from the fridge
- 50 g water
- 50 g bread flour

### Dough:
- 300 g water
- 100 g active starter, doubled in size
- 10 g kosher salt (plain sea salt or table salt also works)
- 450 g bread flour, about 12.7% protein (King Arthur Bread Flour)

### Shaping and Baking:
- Olive oil or vegetable oil, to grease the bowl
- Bread flour, to dust the banneton and the dough
- Parchment paper, a little bigger than the dough

## Instructions:
### Feed the Starter (evening before):
1. Take 25 g of unfed starter out of the fridge.
2. Add 50 g water and stir until the starter dissolves into the water.
3. Add 50 g bread flour and stir to a pancake batter consistency.
4. Scrape the sides of the jar down. Mark the top of the starter with a rubber band so you can see the rise.
5. Put a loose lid on the jar, or use a breathable lid.
6. Leave it on the counter 6 to 8 hrs. The starter must double in size.

### Mix the Dough (next morning):
1. Set a mixing bowl on a digital scale and weigh everything into it. Volume measures will not work here.
2. Add 300 g water. This gives a 70% hydration dough.
3. Add 100 g active starter. You fed 100 g, so 25 g is left over — put that back in the fridge for the next bake.
4. Add 10 g salt.
5. Break it all up with a spoon and stir until the starter and the salt dissolve and the water goes milky.
6. Add 450 g bread flour.
7. Stir with a metal or wooden spoon, 1 to 2 mins, until the dry flour is gone and the dough holds as a shaggy lump. Metal does not hurt sourdough.
8. Cover with a kitchen towel and rest 30 mins at room temperature. The flour hydrates and the dough holds together better.

### Stretch and Fold (three sets):
1. Stretch one edge of the dough up into the air and fold it over the top.
2. Quarter turn the bowl. Stretch and fold the next edge.
3. Repeat for four edges total. That is one set.
4. Cover and rest 30 mins.
5. Do set two the same way. The dough will feel smoother and stretchier.
6. Cover and rest 30 mins.
7. Do set three the same way. The gluten is now developed.

### Bulk Ferment:
1. Oil a 7-cup glass bowl lightly. A clear bowl lets you watch the rise.
2. Transfer the dough in seam side down, smooth side up.
3. Lid on, or plastic wrap for an airtight seal.
4. Rise at room temperature 4 to 8 hrs, until the dough is about double.

### Pre-Shape:
1. Turn the dough out onto an unfloured counter.
2. Cup and pull the dough with your hands to build tension across the top. Work it into a tight ball.
3. Rest uncovered 30 mins. The dough relaxes and the final shape gets easier.
4. Dust a 10-inch oval banneton generously with bread flour. Set it aside.

### Final Shape:
1. Dust the top of the dough with bread flour.
2. Flip the dough so the floured side is down and the sticky side is up.
3. Pull the edges out to make a rectangle instead of a circle.
4. Divide the dough into thirds in your head. Fold the right third over the middle and press it down.
5. Fold the left third over the middle and press it down. You now have one long seam down the middle.
6. Pinch or pop any big air bubbles.
7. Roll the dough into a tight cylinder, starting at the far end and rolling toward you. Press with your thumbs as you go.
8. Smooth the ends. Stretch the end flap out, tuck it under the loaf, and repeat on the other side.
9. Flip the dough into the banneton seam side up, smooth side down.
10. Pinch closed any spot where the seam opens.

### Final Proof:
1. Cover with a kitchen towel and rise 1 hr at room temperature. The dough gets a head start and feels puffy.
2. Pick one:
   - Rise 30 more mins, then bake.
   - Cold proof in the fridge 8 to 24 hrs, then bake whenever you want the next day.

### Bake:
1. 30 mins before baking, heat the oven to 450°F. Put the Dutch oven in to preheat with the oven.
2. Take the dough out of the fridge. Bake it cold. Do not let it come to room temperature.
3. Loosen the dough from the sides of the banneton with your fingers.
4. Lay the parchment paper on top of the dough and flip it so the dough drops out onto the paper, right side up.
5. Brush off the excess flour on top.
6. Score with one single slash. Start at the top, hold the blade at a shallow angle, and slash all the way down. This makes the ear.
7. Lower the dough into the hot Dutch oven, parchment and all.
8. Lid on. Bake 20 mins at 450°F. The steam is trapped.
9. Lid off. Bake 15 mins for a light brown loaf, or 20 mins for a dark crispy crust.
10. Move the loaf to a cooling rack and cool at least 1 hr before slicing. Cutting early ruins the crumb.

